TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit provides a consistent local solar time, but does not keep the spacecraft continuously illuminated. The claim that the descending path is constantly in sunlight is unsupported because the local time of the ascending or descending node is not specified."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says RAAN indicates the launch-facility location. RAAN describes the inertial orientation of the orbital plane; achieving the desired plane requires launch timing and injection geometry. The stated elements also lack a clear epoch."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"No sensor, spatial or spectral resolution, swath, or revisit analysis demonstrates that a 600 km satellite can produce useful fluid-lensing reef images or cover the proposed areas at the stated cadence."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant is identified as a radio observatory, but the report does not establish spacecraft TT&C/data-link capability, compatible frequencies, antenna availability, or operational support there."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The drag sail is proposed without sizing, a predicted decay/re-entry time, or evidence that the disposal plan meets an applicable debris-mitigation timeline."},{"where":"Mission Design","issue":"The report presents STK outputs but does not include the requested relevant STK project files in the submitted material."}],"overall":"A coherent preliminary Australian reef-monitoring concept with useful STK access analysis and broad coverage of required mission sections. Marks are limited by unverified payload feasibility, incomplete operational and disposal justification, a few orbital-geometry misconceptions, and presentation and referencing weaknesses.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The reef-monitoring purpose, Australian benefit, rationale for one spacecraft, and likely science/government customers are explained. The discussion of bleaching and monitoring need is detailed. However, no specific organisation is identified as the spacecraft operator, and the customer/operator roles remain vague.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite, nicknamed ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, orbit, ground access, duration, and disposal, with STK images and access results. The orbital inclination is plausible for a 600 km sun-synchronous orbit. However, payload capability and reef imaging performance are not established; the local-time-of-node assumption is missing; ground-station compatibility is unverified; and the drag-sail disposal performance is not quantified. STK project files are not provided in the submission.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"the proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected launch, orbit, and ground facility and relates the mission to reef protection. It is relevant, but mostly summarizes earlier choices rather than drawing detailed conclusions from demonstrated imaging or operational performance.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognisable engineering structure, executive summary, headings, conclusion, and bibliography, and the figures support the discussion. Presentation is not fully professional: figures and tables lack consistent numbered captions, several text and grammar issues remain, the bibliography entries are incomplete, and table citations such as [49]–[74] do not match the listed references. Wikipedia is also used despite the task disallowing it.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Image 4","quote":""},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight."}]}]}
